WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.000
Loki's been an integral villain and eventual&nbsp;
anti-hero in the Marvel Cinematic Universe for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.760
years. While he's made his mark in more obvious&nbsp;
ways, From inside jokes to crazy comic references,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.800
here are the best Loki Easter eggs in the MCU.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:21.600
After seemingly falling to his doom in Thor, Loki&nbsp;
reappears as the primary villain of The Avengers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.880
Or at least, he seems to be. He's actually&nbsp;
leading an invasion of Earth on behalf of Thanos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.280
laying the groundwork for Avengers:&nbsp;
Infinity War six years down the road.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.840
Thanos relays his messages to Loki through&nbsp;
a hooded character called the Other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.240
who wants to make sure Loki understands&nbsp;
how seriously Thanos takes his mission.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:49.920
"You think you know pain? He will make&nbsp;
you long for something sweet as pain."

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.480
While the Other delivers his menacing monologue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.920
a snake-like creature slithers&nbsp;
meaningfully in the background.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.400
Loki doesn't need to be told twice. In the&nbsp;
real-life collection of Norse mythological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:05.520
poems known as the Elder Edda, the gods&nbsp;
punish Loki for killing Odin's favorite son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:10.880
Baldur, by tying him to a rock with his own&nbsp;
kid's intestines while a snake hangs above him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.120
dripping venom onto his face.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:17.680
Loki's wife, Sigyn, catches the toxins&nbsp;
in a basin, but every once in a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:22.800
she has to leave to empty it, and the venom&nbsp;
falls on Loki's face so painfully that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.640
shakes the whole Earth. The snake in&nbsp;
Avengers may have been a reminder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.880
both to Loki and us, of just how&nbsp;
much pain he's endured in the past.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.720
In the end of The Avengers, the heroes save&nbsp;
the day and capture Loki so Thor can extradite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:42.240
him to his home in Asgard to face Odin's&nbsp;
justice. As Thor carries his brother away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.400
he's locked up not just with cuffs on&nbsp;
his wrist but a muzzle on his mouth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.720
That seems fitting for a character who&nbsp;
does so much damage with just his words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.840
and it's even more fitting when you&nbsp;
know a little about your mythology.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:02:00.640
In medieval historian Snorri Sturluson's Younger&nbsp;
Edda, Loki thinks it'd be a good idea to shave all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.440
of Sif's hair. When the other gods catch him,&nbsp;
Loki agrees to commission the dwarfs to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:10.880
Sif more hair out of gold. Loki offers to pay&nbsp;
with his own head, and the dwarfs get to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:16.880
making not just making a headdress for Sif but&nbsp;
also a spear for Odin and Thor's hammer, Mjolnir.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:22.560
When it comes time to pay, Loki agrees, but he&nbsp;
reminds the dwarfs the deal is only for his head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.800
and if the dwarfs take off any of his&nbsp;
neck along with it, the bargain is void.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:31.760
The dwarfs have no choice but to let Loki go,&nbsp;
but they still get back at him by sewing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.640
mouth shut to keep him from smooth-talking&nbsp;
anyone else again. Loki's muzzle in The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.880
Avengers looks a lot less painful than&nbsp;
that, but the parallel should be clear.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:45.920
In Thor: The Dark World, Thor and Loki&nbsp;
commandeer one of the Dark Elves' ships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:52.080
from the Asgardian impound. As Thor and Loki flee&nbsp;
Asgard, Thor sideswipes one of Asgard's monuments.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.320
"Well done, you've just&nbsp;
decapitated your grandfather."

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:03.360
It's a brief throwaway line, but comics&nbsp;
fans may recognize its deeper significance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:10.400
In 2009's Thor #600, Thor really does kill his&nbsp;
own grandfather — thanks to Loki, of course. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:15.360
god of mischief travels thousands of years into&nbsp;
the past to trap the soul of Thor's grandfather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.240
Bor, in the snow, then returns to resurrect&nbsp;
him ... but not before scrambling Bor's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:25.680
mind so he'll attack everything in sight. As&nbsp;
Bor rampages through New York, Thor finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:31.120
realizes there's no way to stop him except by&nbsp;
killing him — all according to Loki's plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.640
since the penalty for killing&nbsp;
Asgardian royalty is exile.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:40.240
Marvel loves sneaking in Easter eggs so much&nbsp;
that they're not content to stop with the movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.880
Even the tracklist to Thor: The Dark World&nbsp;
manages to squeeze in a reference that goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:50.640
back beyond Loki's comic book history to the&nbsp;
original Norse texts. Composer Brian Tyler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:55.120
includes Loki's theme on the album, named&nbsp;
"Lokasenna." It may seem like a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:59.760
random letters attached to Loki's name at first&nbsp;
glance, but it actually comes from one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:05.200
earliest surviving references to the character,&nbsp;
an Old Norse poem collected in the Elder Edda.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:10.000
Even in the Middle Ages, the Loki we know and&nbsp;
love was already fully formed. The poem tells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:13.840
of him getting kicked out of a party&nbsp;
for killing another one of the guests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.520
then returning to pick fights with other gods and&nbsp;
accusing them of all kinds of crimes. Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:24.160
Loki politely excuses himself when Thor&nbsp;
threatens to shut him up with his hammer.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.960
The end of The Dark World reveals that Loki&nbsp;
didn't actually die in his battle with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:34.480
Dark Elves. In fact, the Odin that Thor has been&nbsp;
asking permission to return to Earth is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:39.280
Loki in disguise. So, when Thor returns from&nbsp;
his journeys across the universe in search of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:44.400
the Infinity Stones in Thor: Ragnarok, he gets&nbsp;
to see what Loki has been up to in his absence.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:49.840
Still pretending to be Odin, Loki is putting on a&nbsp;
play that tells the story of his supposed heroic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:54.880
death, with Chris Hemsworth's brother,&nbsp;
Luke, as Thor and Matt Damon as Loki.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:59.760
Before we see what's going on, we get a brief&nbsp;
look at the stage itself as Thor flies over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:04.640
Asgardian skyline. The design fits the vanity&nbsp;
of the whole project — the whole thing's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:09.840
gigantic recreation of Loki's own golden&nbsp;
helmet with its distinctive curved horns.

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:15.280
In Thor: Ragnarok, Loki's self-congratulatory play&nbsp;
proves that he's probably better off sticking with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:21.040
supervillainy than writing. As Matt Damon plays&nbsp;
Loki's drawn-out death scene, he uses his last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:26.480
words to atone for all his regrets, including&nbsp;
the events of The Avengers — and another thing.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:29.440
"Sorry about that time I turned you into a frog."

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:34.080
Surely something that silly couldn't have been&nbsp;
an actual story in the comics, could it? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:39.760
in the '80s, writer-artist Walt Simonson took the&nbsp;
reins of Thor with an appropriately grandiose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:45.440
mythic take on the character, one full of new&nbsp;
worlds, epic battles, and poignant deaths.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:49.120
But he wasn't above a little change&nbsp;
of pace to lighten things up a bit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:54.960
In Thor #364, Loki's latest power&nbsp;
play involves transforming his brother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:59.600
into an amphibian. Thor doesn't let that stand&nbsp;
in the way of his heroism, though, and he aids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:04.400
a colony of Central Park frogs in their war&nbsp;
against the rats before returning to Asgard.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:09.840
Thor: Ragnarok takes a much more lighthearted&nbsp;
approach towards the apocalypse than Norse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:15.600
mythology does, but the film does deliver on the&nbsp;
destruction of Asgard. Toward the end of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.080
things look grim for our heroes. But that's&nbsp;
when Loki shows up in a massive ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:26.320
accompanied by warriors. As Asgardian refugees&nbsp;
flee Hela and seek refuge on Loki's new ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:28.960
the god of mischief welcomes them aboard.

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:34.080
Interestingly, the Younger Edda also says Loki&nbsp;
will arrive at Ragnarok at the helm of a ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:39.360
In Norse mythology, Loki comes bringing&nbsp;
destruction instead of salvation, but Thor:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:42.240
Ragnarok's writer-director, Taika Waititi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:47.360
cleverly flips the script to suit his more&nbsp;
sympathetic interpretation of the character.

